WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.030
Picture the print shop's print line.

2
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:08.072
A job goes in as a file and comes out as paper.

3
00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:10.531
On the way, machines change every job.

4
00:00:10.531 --> 00:00:14.673
One takes off the shrink-wrap, so a cover sheet can go in front.

5
00:00:14.673 --> 00:00:16.874
One adds a blank form at the back.

6
00:00:16.874 --> 00:00:19.657
One clips on a slip that describes the job.

7
00:00:19.657 --> 00:00:22.245
Each acts on a note on the job's ticket.

8
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:26.199
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:28.309
I put an annotation on my class.

10
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:30.748
Isn't the annotation what changes it?

11
00:00:30.748 --> 00:00:32.132
And if not, who does?

12
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.204
A framework is a library that calls your code.

13
00:00:36.204 --> 00:00:43.387
Many frameworks add behavior with a proxy: a class they generate that extends yours and overrides each method.

14
00:00:43.387 --> 00:00:50.244
A timing proxy starts a clock, calls your original print through super, and writes down how long it took.

15
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:55.140
Let's write that subclass by hand, for the shop's PrintQueue.

16
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.182
Does it compile?

17
00:00:56.182 --> 00:00:57.224
Pause and guess.

18
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.155
It doesn't.

19
00:01:01.155 --> 00:01:08.155
Kotlin says this type is final, so it cannot be extended, and print is final and cannot be overridden.

20
00:01:08.155 --> 00:01:12.409
Java hits the same wall: cannot inherit from final PrintQueue.

21
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.067
To be fair, that's a good default: a Kotlin class behaves exactly as its author wrote it.

22
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:23.524
On the print line, a final class is a job that arrives shrink-wrapped.

23
00:01:23.524 --> 00:01:26.007
Nobody can slip a cover sheet in front.

24
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:31.055
You could write open on every class and every method a framework touches.

25
00:01:31.055 --> 00:01:35.792
By the third class, someone forgets one, and finds out while the program starts.

26
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:39.378
So what if we just add a note to the ticket?

27
00:01:39.378 --> 00:01:43.276
An annotation, OpenForProxies, and nothing else in the build.

28
00:01:43.276 --> 00:01:45.512
What does javap show for the class?

29
00:01:45.512 --> 00:01:46.535
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:51.426
Still public final class.

31
00:01:51.426 --> 00:01:54.811
An annotation is just a label that tools can read.

32
00:01:54.811 --> 00:01:58.671
A note on a ticket does nothing until a machine reads it.

33
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:07.360
That machine is a compiler plugin: a jar that the Kotlin compiler loads to change what it writes into class files.

34
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.085
all-open opens marked classes and their members, with no open keyword.

35
00:02:12.085 --> 00:02:15.324
You name the annotation once, in the build file.

36
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:23.268
Now javap tells a different story: public class PrintQueue, and a public print, with no final in sight.

37
00:02:23.268 --> 00:02:31.077
The timing subclass compiles and runs, and the demo prints: printed flyer.pdf from a tray of 500 sheets.

38
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.587
What the machine leaves alone matters just as much.

39
00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.714
The private method stays private.

40
00:02:36.714 --> 00:02:39.678
The field behind traySize stays private final.

41
00:02:39.678 --> 00:02:44.319
And PlainHelper, with no note on its ticket, stays a public final class.

42
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.590
The second machine adds a blank form at the back.

43
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:59.870
A library that loads records from a database builds your object in two steps: first an empty object, from a constructor with no parameters, then it fills in each field.

44
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:05.779
The no-arg plugin generates that constructor for marked classes, like the shop's JobRow.

45
00:03:05.779 --> 00:03:07.189
Kotlin can't call it.

46
00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:08.800
Java and reflection can.

47
00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:13.120
JobRow declares status, a String, with the value NEW.

48
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.436
Build a blank one the way the library does.

49
00:03:16.436 --> 00:03:17.746
What's in status?

50
00:03:17.746 --> 00:03:18.980
Pause and guess.

51
00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:22.505
null.

52
00:03:22.505 --> 00:03:27.857
The generated constructor is three instructions, and none of them sets a field.

53
00:03:27.857 --> 00:03:30.838
The demo prints: blank.status is null: true.

54
00:03:30.838 --> 00:03:34.768
A paper form would at least have status NEW printed on it.

55
00:03:34.768 --> 00:03:38.968
So don't trust a field of an object you didn't build yourself.

56
00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:42.345
The third machine clips on a slip.

57
00:03:42.345 --> 00:03:51.159
Mark a class Serializable, and the serialization plugin writes a whole new class while your code compiles: Queued$$serializer.

58
00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:59.763
It lists the fields one by one, so a job event can be written out as JSON text and read back, with no guessing at run time.

59
00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:02.554
The demo saves two events and prints this.

60
00:04:02.554 --> 00:04:06.373
The type field tells a reader which kind of event each one is.

61
00:04:06.373 --> 00:04:12.595
Pin its value with SerialName, or moving a class to another package quietly changes your saved files.

62
00:04:13.567 --> 00:04:15.327
And here the picture breaks.

63
00:04:15.327 --> 00:04:18.346
On a real line, you can watch the machines work.

64
00:04:18.346 --> 00:04:23.377
Your source shows none of it: the class you read is not the class the JVM loads.

65
00:04:23.377 --> 00:04:30.105
So when something surprises you, javap shows what's really there, and the build file says who put it there.

66
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:32.146
So, three rules.

67
00:04:32.146 --> 00:04:37.248
An annotation is only a note: check that a plugin named in the build reads it.

68
00:04:37.248 --> 00:04:40.125
A blank constructor skips your initializers.

69
00:04:40.125 --> 00:04:44.311
And after every change to the build, check one class with javap.

70
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.237
One more family, for later.

71
00:04:47.237 --> 00:04:50.466
kapt and KSP don't change class files at all.

72
00:04:50.466 --> 00:04:56.566
They write new source files from your notes, like a second shop printing extra pages.

73
00:04:56.566 --> 00:04:59.364
Part 5 of this course is all about KSP.

74
00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.016
So, who changes my class?

75
00:05:02.016 --> 00:05:03.295
Not the annotation.

76
00:05:03.295 --> 00:05:05.921
The annotation is a note on the ticket.

77
00:05:05.921 --> 00:05:10.499
A compiler plugin, named in the build, is the machine that reads it.

78
00:05:10.499 --> 00:05:15.279
It takes away a final, adds a constructor, or writes a whole new class.

79
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:16.337
Quick check.

80
00:05:16.337 --> 00:05:20.455
A module has the serialization library, but not the plugin.

81
00:05:20.455 --> 00:05:24.503
You mark Queued Serializable and call Queued.serializer().

82
00:05:24.503 --> 00:05:26.108
What happens, and when?

83
00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.498
It fails at compile time: unresolved reference serializer.

84
00:05:33.498 --> 00:05:34.801
The note is there.

85
00:05:34.801 --> 00:05:36.104
The machine isn't.
